@zyeborm  I don't think that will ever take off. Way too much power is generated with AC and consumed as AC. And DC transmission lines only benefit when you are combining two different AC grids that are not synchronized or when the capacitive losses far outweigh the resistive losses. Otherwise, AC goes much further than DC. 

It Would solve power factor issues but it does not fix harmonics, noise. All your devices would use buck converters amd as a result of the sharp spikes of high current, you will get inductive spikes that  will propagate as high voltage spikes down the wire. It's also much harder on switches both solid state and physical contacts. It's hard to break a DC arc so you get alot more heat anywhere it does.  Where AC has  zero potential crossing that exstinguishes arcs in electrical contacts. Then the high voltage spikes fro. Switching DC loads that have inductance or from connecting something with a large capacitance would be very hard on solid state switches. AC solves this. And it's not terribly exspensive to correct power factor. 

Adding capacitors to large motors or new motors, that enharently have better power factor, and useing motor drives that both correct power factor but also save energy on the application are already very popular solutions. Adding an active front end to switchmode powersupplies will be popular. Especially since they represent are a minority of the bulk of the power used.. popular in homes. Not popular in industry, it's just motors that use so much more power than anything else

