There really isn't much on mains that is too likely to cause problems with digital circuitry unless there is something quite unusual. I've seen some pretty hideous mains noise caused by an electric arc furnace used for metal smelting. It IS sometime necessary and always wise to provide protection against transient voltages that may occur on mains, but this is done with devices like gas discharge tubes, metal oxide varistors and the like. Inductance can be beneficial because it slows the rate of change of current so narrow spikes are easier to suppress.

The vast majority of EMI/RFI filtering, whether it is on the mains input or signal connections in digital circuity is to keep the noise IN rather than OUT. Switched mode power supplies can put a lot or RF on the AC mains unless proper filtering is used. Lots of RF noise can be carried out of shielding enclosure on signal cables. The ferrites that are sometimes found on things like video cables are to keep noise from going out on the cables, not to keep external noise from interfering with the signals.

